#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""sharedbox-trap: decoy ports, bait URLs and an AI-crawler maze that waste attackers' time.

  sharedbox-trap serve                    the daemon (sharedbox-trap.service, runs as `sharedbox`)
  sharedbox-trap publish PORT             put the web app on 127.0.0.1:PORT on the public internet
                                          (Tailscale Funnel -> trap proxy -> app)          [root]
  sharedbox-trap unpublish PORT           take it off the internet                         [root]
  sharedbox-trap stats [--days N]         our wins: bombs delivered, hours wasted, bots mazed

Decoy ports (TRAP_PORTS, bound to the LAN + Tailscale addresses only, never 127.0.0.1):
  speaks HTTP  -> gzip bomb: ~10 MB on the wire, TRAP_BOMB_GB of zeros once the client inflates it
  anything else -> tarpit: an endless pre-banner, one line every ~10 s, for up to TRAP_TARPIT_HOURS
  Nothing legitimate ever connects to these, so a hit from inside your network (LAN/tailnet)
  means something there is scanning: sharedbox-health emails you about it.

Published apps go through the trap proxy, which decides per request:
  bait URL (/.env, /wp-login.php, ...)          -> gzip bomb (HTTP tarpit when over the bomb budget)
  /robots.txt                                   -> ours: AI crawlers disallowed, /archive/ off-limits
  AI crawler User-Agent, or an IP that fetched
  the forbidden /archive/ (ignored robots.txt)  -> endless slow link maze of generated babble
  everything else                               -> the real app (websockets included)
"""

